Serious audit of everything built across the 9-phase client/worker
restructure, using an automated correctness/simplification review plus
three targeted deep-dives (API-route authorization, migration/backfill
data safety, frontend UX/performance) against the full d040605..6155096
diff. Every finding below was independently verified against the real
code before being fixed — nothing here is speculative.

## Security — cross-tenant data exposure (real IDOR gaps, now fixed)

- src/lib/chat-status-queries.ts: getTodaysCalls, getRecentCancellations,
  getRunHistory, getActiveRecoveries, and getLeakMapBenchmarkComparison
  all took only an engagementId sourced from the chat model's tool-call
  input, with no check that it belonged to the requesting user's
  workspace — a user could get the model to echo back (or supply
  directly) another workspace's engagementId and read that client's
  booking roster, run history/error messages, or active win-back
  prospects. All five now verify workspace ownership first, same pattern
  chat-skill-trigger.ts's mutating tools already used correctly.
- src/lib/chat-credentials.ts: checkCredentialAvailability took a
  workspaceId parameter but never used it in its existing-credential
  check — cross-tenant "does this client already have X connected"
  disclosure. Now verifies ownership before answering.

## Correctness bugs (introduced this project, now fixed)

- src/lib/chat-pile-on.ts: the win-back-exit call omitted
  recovery_automation_id (present on every other real call site of
  exitWinBackSequence) — for ActiveCampaign clients, manually enrolling
  a prospect in Pile-On marked the DB row "rebooked" but silently left
  them enrolled in the win-back automation, still receiving its emails.
- src/app/dashboard/engagements/[id]/workers-panel.tsx: updatingWorker
  was a single string tracking only the most-recently-toggled worker as
  busy — toggling worker B while worker A's request was still in flight
  cleared A's disabled state early, allowing a double-submit. Replaced
  with a Set so each worker's busy state is tracked independently.
  Also fixed activeCount's default (states[id] vs states[id] ?? true)
  to match every card's own isEnabled default.

## Honesty fixes — claims that didn't match reality

- src/lib/worker-registry.ts, src/lib/client-profile.ts: both asserted
  "the enablement flow calls getPrimaryDomainForEngagement" as settled
  fact. It doesn't — resolveClientProfileFact has zero real callers
  anywhere in src/app or src/components. The resolver itself is correct;
  no UI surface (enable-worker-modal, pin-down's wizard, rep-onboarding)
  actually pre-fills a "derivable" field yet. Comments corrected to
  describe the real, current gap instead of a wired capability that
  isn't there — matches this project's own established discipline
  against claiming unbuilt capability.

## Performance

- src/app/dashboard/page.tsx: Phase 9's worker-overview/primary-
  engagement lookups ran strictly after the existing 10-query
  Promise.all resolved instead of alongside it, adding a fully serial
  extra round trip to every dashboard load. Parallelized as siblings in
  one outer Promise.all.
- src/lib/worker-analytics.ts, src/app/dashboard/analytics/[workerId]/
  page.tsx, src/app/dashboard/reputation-manager/analytics/page.tsx:
  getRepSignalSummary queried every RM signal ever recorded with no time
  window, unlike every other query in the file — tolerable when it was
  one page nothing linked to, not once it renders on 6 different RM
  worker analytics pages. Windowed to the file's own 30-day
  DETAIL_WINDOW_DAYS; page copy updated to say so rather than silently
  changing what "every signal" meant.

## Migration robustness

- scripts/split-multi-engagement-workspaces.ts: one workspace throwing
  mid-batch used to propagate straight to process.exit(1), silently
  abandoning every workspace still queued behind it — and because the
  audit/rollback log was only written after the full loop finished, even
  the workspaces that DID succeed before the crash got no log entry at
  all. Now catches per-workspace, writes the log for whatever succeeded
  regardless of what failed after it, and ends with an explicit
  processed/failed count and per-workspace error detail instead of a
  bare stack trace.

## Verified clean, no action taken

11 API routes (engagement PATCH, minimal-create, workers/[workerId]/
enable, enable-pile-on, rep-findings + trigger, skill-runs detail, and
the rest of teammates/chat's tool handlers) already scope every
engagement-linked read/write to the requesting workspace correctly.
engagementSkills' "no row = enabled" convention is applied consistently
across every real dispatch gate (cron, pollers, chat triggers, webhook
handlers) — no crossed conventions found. 15 other frontend files
(enable-worker-modal, manual-enroll modals, minimal-create pages,
rep-findings-panel, update-credentials-form, worker-card) all have
correct loading/error/disabled states.

Not fixed here, flagged as an open follow-up: the Phase 1 split
script still has no rollback/undo tool — its audit log records what
moved, but reversing a split means hand-writing the reversal from that
log today. No evidence in the repo that this script has been run
against production yet.

